Analytical Procedures | Population pharmacokinetics and PK/PD analyses were conducted based on data from a Phase 2 study (E5501-G000-202) in subjects with CLD and severe thrombocytopenia undergoing active treatment. The number of evaluable subjects with available avatrombopag concentration and platelet count measurements in each cohort was as follows: | Cohort A (1G Tablet) | - Subjects who received a loading dose of 100 mg on Day 1, followed by 20 mg, 40 mg, or 80 mg QD from Day 2 to Day 7; 51 subjects. | Cohort B (2G Tablet) | - Subjects who received a loading dose of 80 mg on Day 1, followed by 10 mg QD on Day 2 to Day 7, or received a loading dose of 80 mg on Day 1, follwed by 20 mg QD on Day 2 to Day 4; 42 subjects (The 2G tablet formulation is the planned commercial avatrombopag formulation, which was used in the later Phase 2 studies and 2 pivotal Phase 3 studies in subjects with CLD and thrombocytopenia). | The population pharmacokinetics and PK/PD analysis of avatrombopag were performed using nonlinear mixed effects modeling (NONMEM). The analysis and reporting were conducted in accordance with the U.S. FDA Guidance on Population Pharmacokinetic Modeling. | Based on inter-individual variability (IIV) and the correlation between inter-individual random variables and covariates in the baseline platelet count model, the demographic covariates assessed in the population pharmacokinetic modeling included age, weight, sex, race, formulation, dose, alanine aminotransferase (ALT), albumin, alkaline phosphatase, aspartate aminotransferase (AST), bilirubin, concomitant proton pump inhibitors, creatinine clearance, and CLD etiology (viral hepatitis, nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, or alcoholic liver disease). | The PK/PD model was used to predict the relationship between avatrombopag plasma concentration and platelet count. The population PK/PD model was a 6-compartment lifespan PK/PD model, consisting of 2 PK compartments and 4 PD compartments. It was composed of 1 precursor production compartment, 2 transit/maturation compartments, and 1 platelet (blood) compartment. The covariates analyzed included age, weight, sex, race, antiplatelet therapy, Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) score, Child-Turcotte-Pugh (CTP) score, and CLD etiology. | Results | Avatrombopag PK is dose-independent; and not significantly affected by age, weight, race, sex, liver function, renal function, CLD etiology, or concomitant proton pump inhibitors. Serum albumin was the only covariate with a statistically significant effect on V/F. | {'title': '', 'columns': ['Results on population PK parameters'], 'rows': [['', '']], 'footnotes': []}
[CPMS-E5501-005R-v1.0] Population Pharmacokinetic and Pharmacokinetic/Pharmacodynamic Analyses of Avatrombopag in Adults With Thrombocytopenia Associated With Liver Disease Prior to an Elective Procedure
Methodology | Population pharmacokinetic analysis of avatrombopag was conducted by pooled data collected from 10 Phase 1 studies in healthy subjects, 2 Phase 2 studies (Study 202 and Study 204), and 2 Phase 3 studies (Study 301 and Study 311) in subjects with CLD and thrombocytopenia. The population exposure-response analysis for platelet count and ddQTcF was conducted by pooled data from Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311. | Model-based analysis consisted of the avatrombopag population pharmacokinetic model and the exposure-response analyses for platelet count and ddQTcF. All models were developed using NONMEM 7.3. Model building and covariate evaluation followed standard methodologies. Except for the PK/PD model for ddQTcF, the final PK and PK/PD models were assessed for appropriateness using goodness-of-fit plots and visual predictive checks (VPC), and were validated using nonparametric bootstrapping. | The PK model was a one-compartment model with simultaneous first-order and zero-order absorption and elimination, with parameters including CL/F, V/F, 1Ka, D1, ALAG, and bioavailability factors (F1) comparing the 1G and 2G tablet formulations to the suspension formulation. Individual post hoc PK parameters were derived using the final population pharmacokinetic model, and these parameters were included in the PK/PD dataset for subsequent PK/PD analysis. | The PK/PD model predicting the relationship between avatrombopag plasma concentration and platelet count was a 6-compartment lifespan PK/PD model, consisting of 2 PK compartments and 4 PD compartments, with a linear slope representing the effect of avatrombopag on platelet count. The PD compartment consisted of 1 precursor production compartment, 2 transit /maturation compartments, and 1 platelet (blood) compartment. The PK/PD model for ddQTcF was a linear model describing the relationship between avatrombopag concentration and ddQTcF measurements. | Results | Population Pharmacokinetic Analysis | The final NONMEM dataset for avatrombopag population PK analysis included 15,515 concentration-time records from 787 subjects. Among these, 1,414 records were from 396 patients with CLD enrolled in Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311. Of the 15,515 concentration-time records, 1,461 were from subjects receiving the suspension formulation, 1,491 were from subjects receiving the 1G tablet formulation, and 12,563 were from subjects receiving the 2G tablet formulation. | The PK analysis population consisted of 787 subjects, with 391 healthy subjects and 396 patients with CLD. Among them, 522 were White, 59 were Black/African American, 29 were Asian (excluding Chinese and Japanese), 74 were Japanese, 34 were Chinese, 29 were Korean, and 35 were of other races. The study population included 524 males and 263 females, with an age range of 18–84 years (median: 47.0 years) and a weight range of 38.5–175 kg (median: 75.3 kg). | The subjects included 242 with viral hepatitis, 45 with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis, 49 with alcoholic hepatitis, and 59 with other conditions. A total of 77 subjects received the oral suspension, 130 subjects received the 1G tablet, and 612 subjects received the 2G tablet. (Study 501-PK-902 used the suspension and 1G tablet, while Study 005 used the 1G and 2G tablets). | The final PK model was a one-compartment model with simultaneous first-order and zero-order absorption and elimination, with parameters including CL/F, V/F, first-order 1Ka, D1, ALAG, and F1 of the 1G and 2G tablet formulations, as well as the BA of the 1G tablet (Lot No. 56789-101) relative to the suspension formulation. The IIV (inter-individual variability) was estimated for CL/F, V/F, Ka, D1, and the two F1 parameters. Additionally, inter-occasion variability was included for the F1 of the 1G and 2G formulations. Ka, D1, ALAG, and the two F1 parameters, as well as the inter-occasion variability of F1 of the 1G and 2G formulations, were fixed to estimates derived from the PK model developed using Phase 1 data only. Combined proportional and additive residual variability for both concentrations earlier than and after 4 hours post-dose were included. Body weight and CLD were included as covariates affecting V/F in the final PK model. | The precision of all parameter estimates in the structural model was deemed acceptable with 95% CI (relative standard errors [RSEs] ≤19.9%). Regarding inter-individual variability, CL/F and V/F had relatively low variability (28.8% and 25.0%, respectively), while Ka had higher variability (137%). The F1 of the 1G and 2G formulations showed moderate variability (35.9%), whereas the F1 of the 1G tablet (Lot No. 56789-101) exhibited higher variability. The inter-occasion variability for F1 of the 1G and 2G formulations was 41.1%, which was considered moderate. The proportional residual variability in avatrombopag concentration ≥4 hours post-administration was low (16.9%), whereas variability in concentrations within 4 hours post-administration was higher (56.3%). | The PK of avatrombopag in healthy subjects and patients with CLD and severe thrombocytopenia was well described by a one-compartment model with first-order and zero-order absorption and linear elimination. The F1 of the 1G and 2G tablets was 74.5% relative to the suspension formulation, while the F1 of the 1G tablet (Lot No. 56789-101) was 21.3% relative to the suspension. The absorption rate constant for the 1G and 2G tablets was 25.8% of that for the suspension formulation. | Although no specific simulation was conducted for avatrombopag 60 mg, similar results would be expected based on the principle of linear PK. | The CL/F of avatrombopag was comparable between healthy subjects and patients with CLD, and avatrombopag exposure, assessed by AUC at the same dose, was also similar between both populations. | Avatrombopag PK was not significantly affected by age (as a continuous or categorical variable), race, sex, renal function (as a continuous or categorical variable), liver function, PT INR, or concomitant administration of antacids, proton pump inhibitors, or H2-receptor antagonists. | Population PK/PD Analysis | Platelet count data were collected from 394 subjects who participated in Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311 and received avatrombopag. The final NONMEM dataset for avatrombopag population PK/PD analysis included 1,877 platelet count-time records from these 394 subjects. | The PK/PD analysis population (including the PK/ddQTcF analysis population) consisted of 394 subjects, among whom 239 were White, 15 were Black/African American, 3 were Asian (excluding Chinese, Japanese, and Korean), 61 were Japanese, 17 were Chinese, 29 were Korean, and 25 belonged to other racial groups. The study population included 264 males and 130 females, with an age range of 19–84 years (median: 58 years) and a weight range of 38.5–175 kg (median: 78.7 kg). The baseline platelet count in the study population ranged from 10.0–107×109/L (median: 39.0×109/L). The subjects included 241 with viral hepatitis, 44 with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), 49 with alcoholic hepatitis, and 59 with other conditions. Additionally, 86 subjects had hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). | A 6-compartment lifespan PK/PD model was applied to the platelet count data collected from patients with CLD in Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311, which consisted of 2 PK compartments and 4 PD compartments, assuming a linear drug effect. Individual post hoc estimates were derived using the final PK model, and these estimates were used as PK exposure input values in the PK/PD model. The PK/PD model consisted of 1 precursor production compartment, 2 transport/maturation compartments, and 1 platelet (blood) compartment. A proportional error model was compared with either combined proportional and additive error term or additive error term. The model parameters included baseline platelet count measurements, slope of drug effect (linear), Kout, and the zero-order production rate of platelet precursors (Kin). Exponential IIV was estimated for all parameters, and combinded residual error terms sum for platelet count was later selected as the base PK/PD model for univariate analysis. | The final PK/PD model for avatrombopag included the effect of East Asian ethnicity (Japanese, Chinese, Korean), plasma thrombopoietin (TPO), and albumin on the slope parameter of drug effect. The precision of all parameter estimates derived from the structural model was high (%RSE ≤18.4%). The IIV estimates also showed high precision (%RSE ≤19.2%). The residual variability estimate for platelet count was highly precise (%RSE ≤8.95%). The estimated parameters from the final PK/PD model were as follows. | The avatrombopag PK/PD model was a 6-compartment lifespan model, with parameters including slope of the linear drug effect, Kin, and Kout. The effect of East Asian ethnicity (Japanese, Chinese, Korean), albumin, and TPO levels on the slope parameter of drug effect was incorporated. | A semi-mechanistic model-based PK/PD analysis showed that the slope parameter was 32% lower (median) in East Asians compared to other populations. However, this difference did not affect the overall change in platelet count from baseline during the scheduled procedure period. Therefore, this difference was considered clinically insignificant. An inverse correlation was observed between albumin and TPO levels and drug effect on platelet count. However, PK/PD model simulations showed that within the observed albumin and TPO range in patients with CLD in Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311, the impact of these covariates on platelet count was minimal and not clinically relevant. | The final PK/PD model was simulated to assess the effect of albumin on platelet count. The baseline albumin concentrations were set at 30, 35, and 40 g/L, which were the most common baseline albumin levels observed in the PK/PD dataset from Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311. The results from a simulation (N=500) in non-East Asian patients with CLD show that the 90% prediction interval (PI) for the platelet count-time profile following avatrombopag administration was similar increases in platelet count across all albumin concentrations and dosing regimens, indicating that albumin had a negligible effect on platelet count increase and was not clinically relevant. | The final PK/PD model was also simulated to assess the effect of TPO increase on platelet count. The baseline TPO concentrations were set at 75, 100, and 40 g/L, which were the most common baseline TPO levels observed in the PK/PD dataset from Study 202, Study 204, Study 310, and Study 311. The results from a simulation in non-East Asian patients with CLD showed that the 90% prediction interval (PI) for the platelet count-time profile following avatrombopag administration was similar across all TPO concentrations and dosing regimens, indicating that TPO had minimal impact on platelet count increase. | Additionally, age, sex, MELD score, CTP score, CLD Etiology, HCC, splenomegaly, and concomitant steroid use did not have a statistically significant effect on any PD parameter. | {'title': '', 'columns': ['Estimated Parameters from the Final PK Model', ['A simulation (N=500) was conducted to assess the impact of weight (50 kg vs. 100 kg) on avatrombopag exposure and platelet count when 40 mg QD was administered for 5 days, assuming a baseline platelet count of 40×109/L.', 'The results showed that while weight and CLD significantly influenced V/F, the PK and PK/PD simulations demonstrated that weight had minimal clinical impact on avatrombopag exposure and platelet count.', 'The predicted exposure and platelet count largely overlapped, confirming that weight had negligible effects on exposure and response.']], 'rows': [['', '']], 'footnotes': []}

